included	w_c05624cc0be4c2cb	A jamming transition from under- to over-parametrization affects generalization in deep learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	717	150	0	0	Title: A jamming transition from under- to over-parametrization affects generalization in deep learning. Claim: The number of unsatisfied constraints jumps discontinuously at the jamming line. Supporting result: The internal discontinuity and behavioral cusp are reproduced across sample sizes, depths, losses, activations, and random versus structured data; early stopping removes the cusp as predicted. Claim: Width and sample count control a qualitative loss-landscape regime change tied to generalization. Supporting result: Systematic parameter/sample sweeps connect the satisfiable/unsatisfiable constraint organization and Hessian geometry to training fit and a cusp in test error across random data and MNIST.
included	w_f94f564014be4c25	A Mechanistic Analysis of Transformers for Dynamical Systems	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	759	147	0	0	Title: A Mechanistic Analysis of Transformers for Dynamical Systems. Claim: A three-dimensional latent state learned from scalar histories preserves the system's predictive dynamics. Supporting result: The compact latent supports held-out forecasting and reconstruction of the higher-dimensional flow field; context and latent-dimension ablations plus five-fold cross-validation establish faithful functional retention. Claim: Context length and latent dimension control recovery of a predictive dynamical-system state. Supporting result: Context, latent-width, parameter-conditioning, and architecture controls connect the change from folded to separated latent geometry to forecasting and flow-field reconstruction over ten seeds and cross-validation folds.
included	w_3f7299052e18118d	A Neural Network Model of Free Recall Learns Multiple Memory Strategies	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1002	185	0	0	Title: A Neural Network Model of Free Recall Learns Multiple Memory Strategies. Claim: The best free-recall models compress list content into a stimulus-invariant serial-index scaffold that supports efficient and robust retrieval. Supporting result: Index explains substantially more hidden-state variance for the memory-palace strategy, cross-decodes across items and study/response phases, and the same recurrent state is the cosine-similarity cue used to retrieve stored memories; the strategy has the best accuracy and noise robustness. Claim: The network learns an item-independent serial-position coordinate system analogous to a memory palace, without a prescribed recall order. Supporting result: Across trained populations, a stable positional trajectory distinct from temporal-context strategies emerges despite reward being indifferent to recall order; it is reinstated during retrieval, linked to forward recall, and its incidence shifts predictably with discount and memory-flush controls.
included	w_07d5eb02f4e65497	A phase transition between positional and semantic learning in a solvable model of dot-product attention	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	838	150	0	0	Title: A phase transition between positional and semantic learning in a solvable model of dot-product attention. Claim: A phase boundary separates positional and semantic attention regimes. Supporting result: At the predicted critical sample load, the positional order parameter gives way to semantic alignment while generalization crosses the positional-baseline boundary; analytic loss-landscape results and repeated finite-size simulations locate the same transition. Claim: Increasing sample complexity shifts attention from positional to semantic solutions. Supporting result: Sweeps over sample load alpha connect a switch in the semantic and positional order parameters to the validation-MSE advantage of the semantic solution; 24 problem instances, 30-run finite-size tests, and matrix/architecture controls reproduce the regimes.
included	w_ee7cdd8c7d281656	A simple model of co-emergence of grid and place fields	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	770	147	0	0	Title: A simple model of co-emergence of grid and place fields. Claim: A single predictive RNN develops complementary grid- and place-like spatial codes without spatial supervision. Supporting result: Neither cell type nor allocentric coordinates are supplied; formal field metrics characterize the learned codes, while architecture removals and new-environment experiments show the recurrent state uses the organization for next-sensory prediction. Claim: Noise, masking, memory decay, environment, and training time reorganize the balance of spatial codes. Supporting result: One thousand parameter configurations, ten-seed replication, architectural controls, and developmental trajectories connect place/grid population regimes to the sensory-prediction computation.
included	w_92e78fbf258ed40a	Assign and Add: A Mechanistic Study of Compositional Arithmetic	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	720	129	0	0	Title: Assign and Add: A Mechanistic Study of Compositional Arithmetic. Claim: The transformer learns to compose a Fourier addition circuit with a variable-binding circuit. Supporting result: The combined mechanism is not prescribed, is formally decomposed into Fourier and modified-induction operations, and isolated-subcircuit, mismatch, and intervention tests establish functional composition on unseen combinations. Claim: Assignment and modular-addition mechanisms form in distinct training phases. Supporting result: Checkpoint analysis and alternative-initialization controls show a modular-addition circuit forming before a sharp variable-binding attention reorganization and the compositional accuracy increase.
included	w_7642d8c74152ab9f	Attention-Head Binding as a Term-Conditioned Mechanistic Marker of Accessibility Concept Emergence in Language Models	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	663	134	0	0	Title: Attention-Head Binding as a Term-Conditioned Mechanistic Marker of Accessibility Concept Emergence in Language Models. Claim: A parameter threshold near 1B and a training-step threshold govern decoupling. Supporting result: The claimed thresholds pair a mechanistic binding transition with behavior, causal top/random/bottom-head controls, five CRFM seeds, and cross-architecture checks. Claim: The structure-behavior relationship transforms qualitatively across scales. Supporting result: Seven models across five architectures and checkpoints show scale- and training-dependent binding regimes; targeted head ablation reverses sign between 160M and 2.8B.
included	w_3d415e237f7c4f65	Autocorrelations from emergent bistability in homeostatic spiking neural networks on neuromorphic hardware	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	863	171	0	0	Title: Autocorrelations from emergent bistability in homeostatic spiking neural networks on neuromorphic hardware. Claim: An input-controlled nonequilibrium transition produces bistability and long autocorrelation times. Supporting result: The internal population distribution changes near the identified input boundary together with the autocorrelation regime; hardware, finite-size simulation, analytic mean field, and repeated experimental runs distinguish the bifurcation from scale-free avalanche criticality. Claim: External input and system size control an emergent bistable population regime after homeostatic learning. Supporting result: Input and size sweeps connect the learned recurrent-weight organization and unimodal-to-bimodal population dynamics to autocorrelation behavior in neuromorphic hardware, software replication, and mean-field controls.
included	w_3144bdd83cd17ac0	Automated construction of cognitive maps with visual predictive coding	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	786	142	0	0	Title: Automated construction of cognitive maps with visual predictive coding. Claim: Predictive coding compresses visual sequences into a compact spatial representation. Supporting result: The predictive latent retains substantially more positional information than a matched autoencoder in visually degenerate corridors, and its learned overlap code is directly consumed to predict future images and decode displacement. Claim: A coordinate-free cognitive map emerges from visual predictive coding. Supporting result: Without position labels or prescribed map coordinates, the model learns formally characterized place-like regions and a metric/topological overlap code; prediction and displacement readouts use that code, with autoencoder and symmetry controls delimiting the result.
included	w_0ff86e6a31e1d145	Balancing Stability and Flow in Hippocampal Networks via Inductive Bias and Learned Symmetry Breaking	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	990	188	0	0	Title: Balancing Stability and Flow in Hippocampal Networks via Inductive Bias and Learned Symmetry Breaking. Claim: Prediction training produces a tilted Mexican-hat recurrent organization from random connectivity. Supporting result: Without prescribing the learned recurrent weights, optimization produces a formally decomposed mixed-symmetry attractor-like organization; replay from noise and prefix-conditioned prediction use its stabilizing symmetric and flow-inducing antisymmetric components, with component-family and local-plasticity controls reproducing the functional regimes. Claim: Replay and prediction depend qualitatively on the balance between symmetric and antisymmetric recurrence. Supporting result: Training trajectories and initialization sweeps connect convergence toward an intermediate symmetry regime to coherent replay, prediction, and optimization; identity, cyclic-shift, random, and Mexican-hat families delimit stabilization and directional-flow alternatives.
included	w_47f0ccb3e2ec3eac	Beyond Induction Heads: In-Context Meta Learning Induces Multi-Phase Circuit Emergence	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	602	113	0	0	Title: Beyond Induction Heads: In-Context Meta Learning Induces Multi-Phase Circuit Emergence. Claim: Circuit metrics change abruptly with the sudden jumps in accuracy. Supporting result: Circuit metrics shift cleanly at behavioral phase boundaries across plotted seeds, including a smooth-loss setting with hidden abrupt circuit specialization. Claim: A unique circuit emerges in each phase during training. Supporting result: Checkpoint curves show NCC, SCC, and FCC circuits replacing one another as ICL accuracy moves through its three plateaus; masked-circuit predictions reproduce phase behavior.
included	w_63db75cd5e03c2b8	Bridging Lottery Ticket and Grokking: Understanding Grokking from Inner Structure of Networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	977	177	0	0	Title: Bridging Lottery Ticket and Grokking: Understanding Grokking from Inner Structure of Networks. Claim: The winning subnetwork changes rapidly at the generalization transition. Supporting result: Graph-structure and overlap quantities transition with accuracy across replicated settings; edge-popup selection converts memorizing weights toward the generalizing behavior without ordinary weight training. Claim: Training discovers a formally structured Fourier-like lottery-ticket circuit. Supporting result: The sparse circuit is not architecturally specified, is characterized by graph and frequency structure, transfers across matched runs, and causally supports generalization through masking and edge-popup interventions. Claim: A sparse generalizing subnetwork forms late in grokking training. Supporting result: Checkpoint, task, architecture, and initialization comparisons connect a structural subnetwork transition to delayed generalization across multiple seeds.
included	w_584c8d983c92b47f	Circuit Synchronization Precedes Generalization: A Causal Precursor to Grokking	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	728	135	0	0	Title: Circuit Synchronization Precedes Generalization: A Causal Precursor to Grokking. Claim: Training constructs a synchronized Fourier circuit that executes the learned algorithm. Supporting result: The circuit is not inserted by the architecture, is formally decomposed into interacting frequency components, and is validated by component-level interventions and forked training controls that change generalization. Claim: Fourier-circuit synchronization develops before delayed generalization. Supporting result: Checkpoint trajectories across tasks, configurations, and seeds connect synchronization of independently learned Fourier components to the later accuracy transition; matched weight-decay forks move both events.
included	w_679c49d202c94f8e	Control of criticality and computation in spiking neuromorphic networks with plasticity	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	794	146	0	0	Title: Control of criticality and computation in spiking neuromorphic networks with plasticity. Claim: Low external drive moves the learned recurrent network into a critical regime with distinct computational behavior. Supporting result: Branching ratio, autocorrelation, susceptibility, finite-size avalanche cutoff, and complex-task performance change together under the input control; one hundred random-seed runs and hardware/software validation replicate the regime. Claim: External input strength controls critical network organization and task-dependent computation. Supporting result: Input-strength and finite-size series connect plasticity-shaped recurrent dynamics to avalanche, branching, correlation, information, and task-performance changes across hardware and software controls.
included	w_3a9d20d4ea381a66	Critical dynamics in associative memory networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	835	156	0	0	Title: Critical dynamics in associative memory networks. Claim: Plasticity regulation moves the learned associative network into a scale-free avalanche regime while preserving retrieval behavior. Supporting result: An explicit power-law-deviation threshold distinguishes critical from noncritical regimes; mechanism controls, load series, and ten-trial ensembles pair the internal regime with stored-pattern retrieval and delimit the capacity boundary. Claim: Memory load and regulatory mechanism control whether learned coupling structure supports both critical dynamics and associative retrieval. Supporting result: Load sweeps and Hebbian-only, homeostatic, and dynamical-synapse controls connect qualitative coupling/regime changes to reconstruction overlap and capacity; ten independent trials per load replicate the relationship.
included	w_d18730b289f06c03	Critical dynamics in homeostatic memory networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	796	158	0	0	Title: Critical dynamics in homeostatic memory networks. Claim: Homeostatic regulation controls a subcritical-to-critical-or-supercritical transition while memory retrieval evolves. Supporting result: An explicit power-law-error threshold, coupling increments, two sizes, depressive/facilitative synapses, and three pattern structures delimit the internal transition and pair it with retrieval-quality dynamics. Claim: Learning history, coupling, network size, and pattern structure control internal regime organization and associative retrieval. Supporting result: Coupling sweeps, two network sizes, three pattern structures, and learning-time traces connect changes in learned connectivity and avalanche organization to stored-pattern overlap under explicit no-recall and homeostatic controls.
included	w_811a8cd021958e82	Deep Networks Always Grok and Here is Why	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1029	198	0	0	Title: Deep Networks Always Grok and Here is Why. Claim: A phase change in linear-region density precedes the behavioral transition. Supporting result: The internal phase change recurs across practical architectures and delayed-generalization/robustness controls; batch-normalization and initialization/regularization ablations delimit when the paired transition occurs. Claim: Training creates a robust partition with decision-boundary-concentrated linear regions. Supporting result: The learned arrangement differs from the initialized partition, is formally characterized through affine-spline regions, and constitutes the model's input-output map whose migration directly changes robustness and generalization. Claim: Training reorganizes the input-space partition before delayed generalization and robustness. Supporting result: Checkpointed local-complexity trajectories across MLPs, CNNs, ResNets, transformers, and datasets show nonlinear regions migrating from samples toward decision boundaries with the later behavior.
included	w_a6027fec6ac3ab9a	Differential learning kinetics govern the transition from memorization to generalization during in-context learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	722	135	0	0	Title: Differential learning kinetics govern the transition from memorization to generalization during in-context learning. Claim: A sharp diversity threshold switches the active internal solution and behavior. Supporting result: Minimal-model theory predicts the threshold and bimodality; at least twenty and up to roughly 250 seeds per critical setting replicate the rapid switch and distributional controls move it as predicted. Claim: Task diversity controls whether independent memorization or in-context subcircuits dominate. Supporting result: Diversity, context length, initialization, regularization, and data-distribution series connect attention/MLP learning kinetics to the memorization-generalization outcome.
included	w_8af770f5f86d5ac0	Distinct mechanisms underlying in-context learning in transformers	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	917	160	0	0	Title: Distinct mechanisms underlying in-context learning in transformers. Claim: Sharp phase boundaries separate the learned ICL algorithms. Supporting result: Rapid internal circuit changes coincide with behavioral switches and are reproduced by minimal models, theory, and broad multi-seed sweeps around the boundaries. Claim: Transformers discover multiple formally distinct in-context algorithms. Supporting result: The subcircuits are learned rather than assigned, their forward computations are explicitly characterized, and targeted mechanistic tests link each algorithm to its corresponding generalizing behavior. Claim: Task diversity and training time select qualitatively distinct ICL mechanisms. Supporting result: Controlled diversity and checkpoint series move the model through memorizing and multiple generalizing attention/MLP algorithms, paired with in-context behavior across many initializations.
included	w_848983c423304f71	Emergence and Effectiveness of Task Vectors in In-Context Learning: An Encoder Decoder Perspective	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	636	124	0	0	Title: Emergence and Effectiveness of Task Vectors in In-Context Learning: An Encoder Decoder Perspective. Claim: Transformers encode latent tasks into distinct representations used by conditional decoders. Supporting result: A compact task vector replaces demonstrations as the task summary; ablation, prompting, and early-layer finetuning alter both task decodability and ICL performance. Claim: Task encoding and conditional decoding emerge together during training. Supporting result: Training checkpoints, OLMo pretraining checkpoints, and five model scales link separable task encodings to ICL performance and algorithm switching.
included	w_a68de8b27948098c	Emergence and Function of Abstract Representations in Self-Supervised Transformers	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	596	102	0	0	Title: Emergence and Function of Abstract Representations in Self-Supervised Transformers. Claim: Semantic features converge onto low-dimensional manifolds. Supporting result: The learned manifold selectively encodes the dataset hierarchy, and gain-of-function manipulations show that retaining this abstraction changes reconstruction decisions. Claim: Self-supervised transformers form an abstract world model. Supporting result: Masked reconstruction without abstraction labels produces a formally measured compositional manifold; causal gain-of-function tests establish that the model uses it.
included	w_a6ec62ef7863cd72	Emergence of Foveal Image Sampling from Learning to Attend in Visual Scenes	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	858	192	0	0	Title: Emergence of Foveal Image Sampling from Learning to Attend in Visual Scenes. Claim: A learned 12-by-12 retinal lattice compresses a 100-by-100 visual scene by allocating high resolution only where it is useful. Supporting result: The learnable lattice selects a small differentiable glimpse and reallocates resolution; fixing the lattice worsens error from 5.1% to 11.8% on fixed-size digits and from 24.4% to 31.9% on variable-size digits. Claim: A foveated, eccentricity-dependent sampling geometry emerges from an initially uniform lattice without directly prescribing foveation. Supporting result: The Gaussian-kernel lattice smoothly changes from a uniform grid into a formal eccentricity-dependent geometry; allowing zoom suppresses the geometry, while a fixed-lattice control degrades behavior, tying the learned representation to its function.
included	w_ab07437d78318352	Emergence of functional and structural properties of the head direction system by optimization of recurrent neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	787	137	0	0	Title: Emergence of functional and structural properties of the head direction system by optimization of recurrent neural networks. Claim: Unconstrained recurrent training produces Compass and Shifter cell classes with structured connectivity. Supporting result: No ring or shifter organization is wired into the RNN; tuning and connectivity formally characterize the learned circuit, and class-specific connection lesions selectively abolish heading maintenance or clockwise/counterclockwise updating. Claim: Input motion statistics reorganize the learned head-direction circuit. Supporting result: Low, medium, and high angular-velocity training distributions change the Compass/Shifter allocation and tuning while preserving integration behavior across independently trained networks.
included	w_aaa017a83700f80c	Emergence of Invariance and Disentanglement in Deep Representations	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	633	115	0	0	Title: Emergence of Invariance and Disentanglement in Deep Representations. Claim: A minimal sufficient representation is maximally invariant to nuisances. Supporting result: Information-theoretic results prove selective retention of label information while discarding nuisance information, and CIFAR experiments connect the bound to generalization. Claim: Information regularization predicts a sharp transition near the critical value beta equals one. Supporting result: Random-label controls and multiple CNN architectures reproduce the predicted information/accuracy boundary; real-label curves provide the task-relevance control.
included	w_7209270e01aef2a8	Emergent Capabilities Arise Randomly from Learning Sparse Attention Patterns	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	687	122	0	0	Title: Emergent Capabilities Arise Randomly from Learning Sparse Attention Patterns. Claim: A sparse-attention organizational transition coincides with abrupt capability acquisition. Supporting result: The rapid transition replicates across scale and seeds; transplanting post-emergence attention into pre-emergence checkpoints recovers behavior while matched synthetic controls delimit alternatives. Claim: Capability onset follows the training- and scale-dependent appearance of sparse attention patterns. Supporting result: Five Pythia scales, training checkpoints, context-sparsity and head-count controls, and ten seeds per setting connect learned sparse attention to task accuracy.
included	w_4ea8cb6a3b5fed46	Emergent Generalization by Representation Learning in Artificial Neural Networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	712	126	0	0	Title: Emergent Generalization by Representation Learning in Artificial Neural Networks. Claim: A learned information bottleneck retains the task structure needed for generalization. Supporting result: The bottleneck representation is lower-dimensional than the input, selectively preserves rotation-relevant information, and its causal-emergence score predicts and mediates robust held-out performance. Claim: Representation learning undergoes a control-dependent emergence trajectory. Supporting result: Training time, bottleneck size, task complexity, and data controls move the hidden representation between memorizing and rotation-structured regimes while changing held-out and out-of-distribution behavior.
included	w_f6a044308e277e6f	Emergent mechanisms for long timescales depend on training curriculum and affect performance in memory tasks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	809	161	0	0	Title: Emergent mechanisms for long timescales depend on training curriculum and affect performance in memory tasks. Claim: Multi-task training creates a recurrently mediated long-timescale mechanism. Supporting result: The recurrent mechanism is learned rather than wired, is quantitatively separated from prescribed single-neuron time constants, and targeted neuron ablations plus recurrent-weight/time-constant perturbations establish its functional role in memory. Claim: Curriculum and memory demand select distinct learned long-timescale mechanisms. Supporting result: Increasing memory length and changing the single- versus multi-head curriculum reorganize learned neuronal and recurrent timescales together with accuracy, robustness, and retraining behavior across independent networks and two tasks.
included	w_e219fcd05aa4ac72	Emergent Symbol-like Number Variables in Artificial Neural Networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	724	135	0	0	Title: Emergent Symbol-like Number Variables in Artificial Neural Networks. Claim: Networks learn reusable symbol-like number subspaces without symbolic coordinates being supplied. Supporting result: The subspaces have a formally tested variable geometry across architectures, and distributed-alignment-search interchange interventions establish that the model uses them in sequence computations rather than merely making them probe-decodable. Claim: Symbol-like number variables strengthen across architecture, dimension, and task controls. Supporting result: Training and controlled model/task series show a qualitative move from item-bound codes to reusable number-variable subspaces together with compositional behavior.
included	w_cb00fb1bab7f3b7f	Emergent Symbolic Mechanisms Support Abstract Reasoning in Large Language Models	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	914	175	0	0	Title: Emergent Symbolic Mechanisms Support Abstract Reasoning in Large Language Models. Claim: Symbol abstraction heads convert tokens to invariant abstract variables. Supporting result: Token instances are reduced to reusable variables; cross-token decoding exceeds 98%, while causal mediation and controlled ablations establish necessity and sufficiency. Claim: The same three-stage architecture appears in three abstract reasoning tasks. Supporting result: The mechanism recurs across randomized-token rule induction, letter-string analogies, and verbal analogies in 13 models from four families, with random/control ablations. Claim: An emergent three-stage symbolic architecture supports abstract reasoning. Supporting result: Unmodified pretrained transformers learn variable-binding, symbolic-induction, and retrieval stages; representation, attention, patching, and ablation analyses jointly validate use.
included	w_63d2efb7962dd788	Evolving reservoir computers reveal bidirectional coupling between predictive power and emergent dynamics	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	899	168	0	0	Title: Evolving reservoir computers reveal bidirectional coupling between predictive power and emergent dynamics. Claim: A learned macroscopic forecast condenses a 100-neuron microstate while preserving synergistic predictive information. Supporting result: The ridge-trained scalar readout depends on the full reservoir yet contains future information unavailable from individual neurons and is the functional prediction; readout randomization and reciprocal objective optimization link the reduced feature to performance. Claim: Training-set size and optimization objective control the onset of a synergistic predictive macrostate. Supporting result: Data-size and bidirectional objective sweeps move the forecast from non-emergent to positive-synergy organization together with prediction success across six environments and repeated reservoir populations; randomized readouts delimit the effect.
included	w_0daa43451a63b0d5	Excitatory-inhibitory recurrent dynamics produce robust visual grids and stable attractors	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	848	171	0	0	Title: Excitatory-inhibitory recurrent dynamics produce robust visual grids and stable attractors. Claim: Recurrent activity is constrained to low-dimensional ring and toroidal attractors. Supporting result: High-dimensional recurrent activity contracts onto a two-dimensional ring or three-dimensional torus that retains position; the trained decoder uses it, and loss of the ring under speed or architecture controls coincides with unstable positional output. Claim: Grid-like population codes emerge as continuous attractor manifolds. Supporting result: The grid/ring geometry is not prescribed by the architecture or loss, is characterized by rate maps, grid scores, topology, and fixed points, and is used for position prediction; ten-network and cue, scene, activation, Dale, sparsity, and darkness controls establish robustness and function.
included	w_cddfb17d263e3744	Explaining Grokking and Information Bottleneck through Neural Collapse Emergence	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	639	107	0	0	Title: Explaining Grokking and Information Bottleneck through Neural Collapse Emergence. Claim: Population within-class variance bounds redundant representation information. Supporting result: Formal bounds show task-irrelevant information contracts while class information remains sufficient; the same quantity bounds generalization error and matches empirical collapse. Claim: Neural collapse progresses on a later timescale than training-set fitting. Supporting result: Theory and multi-dataset, multi-architecture experiments connect within-class contraction over training to delayed generalization and information-bottleneck dynamics.
included	w_45a112f0ccfe5c13	Feature emergence via margin maximization: case studies in algebraic tasks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	858	150	0	0	Title: Feature emergence via margin maximization: case studies in algebraic tasks. Claim: Maximum-margin solutions are sparse in Fourier or irreducible-representation space. Supporting result: Theorems restrict each neuron to a single feature family while retaining a complete task solution; experiments converge to the predicted sparse representation. Claim: Networks learn Fourier and irreducible group-representation features. Supporting result: Without feature labels, trained weights move from random mixtures to the formally characterized bases that the proofs show are required for the learned computation. Claim: Representations arise as networks approach maximum margin during training. Supporting result: Training trajectories on modular addition, sparse parity, and group composition converge to the predicted internal features and task solutions.
included	w_87462cebc24e3c1e	From movement to cognitive maps: recurrent neural networks reveal how locomotor development shapes hippocampal spatial coding	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	969	177	0	0	Title: From movement to cognitive maps: recurrent neural networks reveal how locomotor development shapes hippocampal spatial coding. Claim: Egocentric sensorimotor prediction produces an allocentric spatial representation. Supporting result: The RNN learns formally measured place/head-direction structure and an allocentric manifold without spatial-coordinate supervision; silencing classified spatial cells raises validation loss across 100 repetitions, and held-out decoding, stability, and experimental-recording comparisons support model use. Claim: Developmental movement statistics drive sequential emergence of spatially tuned units. Supporting result: Crawl, walk, run, and adult trajectory regimes cause progressive spatial organization and improved position/head-direction decoding; matched exposure, reversed development, temporal-gap, capacity, input, and training-duration controls plus lesion effects tie the internal progression to prediction behavior.
included	w_27f0a0911b68ee3a	Gradient-based learning drives robust representations in recurrent neural networks by balancing compression and expansion	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	929	169	0	0	Title: Gradient-based learning drives robust representations in recurrent neural networks by balancing compression and expansion. Claim: Training compresses within-class activity into low-dimensional attractors. Supporting result: The recurrent state selectively collapses within-class variation while preserving class separation used by the trained readout; fixed-point/chaotic-attractor analyses and held-out-cluster tests show preserved or improved task performance, with an explicit setting where compression is not necessary retained as a limitation. Claim: Learning reorganizes recurrent activity through expansion and compression as training samples accumulate. Supporting result: Training-sample trajectories pair changes in effective dimension and attractor organization with classification and held-out-cluster performance across 30 network/input realizations, full/minibatch controls, and additional MNIST experiments.
included	w_35bf58ca4093d76e	Grokking and epoch-wise double descent in quantum neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1098	197	0	0	Title: Grokking and epoch-wise double descent in quantum neural networks. Claim: Generalization uses a sparse phase-aligned harmonic representation in place of the earlier dense solution. Supporting result: The learned output representation retains the task boundary with fewer active Fourier components, while weight-norm control preserves both the sparse state and its generalization benefit. Claim: A rapid internal representation transition accompanies the sudden generalization transition. Supporting result: Fourier sparsification and leave-one-out stability change at the grokking event alongside test error; 128-seed depth and hyperparameter series replicate the paired transition and regularization controls its persistence. Claim: Depth and training time control a dense-to-sparse internal solution change paired with grokking. Supporting result: Depths one through ten and long checkpoint trajectories connect the Fourier reorganization to test-error and stability transitions across 128 independent initializations; regularization and initialization sweeps move or stabilize the regime.
included	w_9db7d6568bb89686	Grokking as a First Order Phase Transition in Two Layer Networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	568	107	0	0	Title: Grokking as a First Order Phase Transition in Two Layer Networks. Claim: Grokking maps to a first-order representation-learning transition. Supporting result: The internal order parameter and performance jump at the same predicted boundary, validated with up to 200 networks and 50 initialization/data seeds. Claim: Sample size, noise, and width control qualitatively different learned features. Supporting result: Analytic order parameters and ensembles of trained networks connect control scaling to internal teacher-feature specialization and generalization.
included	w_8a9381903c1dcd61	Grokking as an entanglement transition in tensor network machine learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	718	140	0	0	Title: Grokking as an entanglement transition in tensor network machine learning. Claim: Eigenvalue evaporation and sharp entropy reduction coincide with grokking. Supporting result: The rapid internal transition recurs across multiple binary classifications and a distinct biological dataset, with initial-volume-law and non-grokking community controls delimiting the behavioral relationship. Claim: Training changes tensor-network representations from volume-law to sub-volume-law organization. Supporting result: Sweep-by-sweep entropy, spectrum, and classification measurements pair the entanglement reorganization with held-out performance across Fashion-MNIST comparisons and independent gene-expression cohorts.
included	w_2b77293cdef4c93f	Grokking as Structural Inference: Transformers Need Bayesian Lottery Tickets	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	708	137	0	0	Title: Grokking as Structural Inference: Transformers Need Bayesian Lottery Tickets. Claim: The transformer learns a task-graph attention structure used for Bayesian inference. Supporting result: The graph is neither wired into attention nor supplied as a label, is characterized by a formal Bayesian model, and KL-targeted intervention changes the learned inference and generalization trajectory. Claim: Attention reorganizes from memorization to structural inference during training. Supporting result: Checkpoint, task-size, and initialization controls show attention condensing onto the latent task graph before generalization; the derived waiting-time law and norm/KL controls track the paired behavior.
included	w_055282d4fa8fa8da	Grokking Beyond the Euclidean Norm of Model Parameters	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	720	134	0	0	Title: Grokking Beyond the Euclidean Norm of Model Parameters. Claim: Grokking selects sparse or low-rank functional solutions from overparameterized alternatives. Supporting result: The learned reduced solutions reconstruct the target rules and preserve held-out accuracy, while matched non-Euclidean regularizers control which compact structure and generalization regime develops. Claim: Training time, regularizer, depth, and data control sparse or low-rank solution formation during grokking. Supporting result: Checkpoint and hyperparameter series connect qualitative parameter organization to the delayed transition from memorization to test generalization across modular-arithmetic and matrix-completion settings.
included	w_cbbadc97bc1c553c	Hippocampal representations emerge when training recurrent neural networks on a memory dependent maze navigation task	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	949	183	0	0	Title: Hippocampal representations emerge when training recurrent neural networks on a memory dependent maze navigation task. Claim: A recurrent network learns an allocentric attractor map and reward-directed trajectory manifold without location or direction labels. Supporting result: The learned state is formally characterized through place fields, attractor geometry, decoding, and forward-sweep trajectories; Q-only and combined-objective controls show that maintaining and reusing the predictive map supports path selection and reward behavior. Claim: Predictive and reward-learning objectives produce distinct hippocampal-like state organizations. Supporting result: Predictive pretraining, combined predictive/Q learning, and Q-only controls reorganize recurrent states from a sensory attractor map into reward-directed sweeps and task-selective activity, with faster successful navigation across ten initial conditions and randomized walls.
included	w_8d71fca6fabfe6c0	How do language models learn facts? Dynamics, curricula and hallucinations	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	702	126	0	0	Title: How do language models learn facts? Dynamics, curricula and hallucinations. Claim: Training constructs an attention-based factual-recall circuit. Supporting result: The token-routing circuit is learned rather than supplied, is characterized by name-grouping and extraction attention, and checkpoint attention patching removes the learning plateau and changes recall acquisition. Claim: Factual learning passes through three phases while recall circuits form during the plateau. Supporting result: Training checkpoints, population size, data distribution, model size, sequence mixer, and hyperparameter controls link attention-circuit development to later knowledge acquisition across five seeds.
included	w_83ff2605b95d13cd	How Do LLMs Acquire New Knowledge? A Knowledge Circuits Perspective on Continual Pre-Training	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	824	141	0	0	Title: How Do LLMs Acquire New Knowledge? A Knowledge Circuits Perspective on Continual Pre-Training. Claim: A checkpoint-localized circuit turning point separates formation from optimization behavior. Supporting result: Circuit entropy and structural consistency change regime at architecture-specific checkpoints; post-transition aligned topologies attain substantially higher standalone recall, and four model families plus relation/frequency controls reproduce the paired shift. Claim: Continual-pretraining time and model scale control a formation-to-optimization change in knowledge circuits. Supporting result: Checkpoint trajectories in four model architectures connect circuit entropy, topology, specialized heads, and layerwise information flow to factual-recall performance under relevance and frequency controls.
included	w_988674ea0e8c1cb7	How Does Controllability Emerge In Language Models During Pretraining?	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	585	100	0	0	Title: How Does Controllability Emerge In Language Models During Pretraining?. Claim: Concept control becomes accessible through low-complexity linear transformations. Supporting result: A reduced linear control representation selectively changes the target concept; intervention and finetuning comparisons tie representation quality to generation behavior. Claim: Linear steerability emerges at distinct stages of pretraining. Supporting result: Checkpoint analyses connect increasing hidden-state separability to the onset of concept steering across model families and concept pairs.
included	w_6d9aedbcf0365751	How Does Unfaithful Reasoning Emerge from Autoregressive Training? A Study of Synthetic Experiments	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	645	113	0	0	Title: How Does Unfaithful Reasoning Emerge from Autoregressive Training? A Study of Synthetic Experiments. Claim: Reasoning faithfulness changes sharply beyond a critical noise threshold. Supporting result: Multiple noise regimes and seeds pair the behavioral threshold with transient entropy and hidden-state differentiation; complexity and shortcut controls support the proposed mechanism. Claim: Training moves models through faithful, mixed, and skip-step reasoning modes. Supporting result: Noise and checkpoint sweeps link hidden-state uncertainty organization to counterfactual faithfulness behavior in controlled synthetic transformers.
included	w_c0722915b2be1f88	How to Tame Grokking: Representation Geometry as a Control Signal	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	878	167	0	0	Title: How to Tame Grokking: Representation Geometry as a Control Signal. Claim: Effective dimensionality stays high, then undergoes a sharp collapse. Supporting result: Low-rank spectral regularization directly reduces hidden dimensionality and preserves or accelerates generalization across target-dimension, timing, architecture, and task sweeps. Claim: Unsmoothed trajectories contain abrupt geometric reorganizations. Supporting result: A narrow training interval contains coordinated layerwise transitions followed by accuracy jumps; direct geometry interventions move transition time reproducibly across seeds. Claim: Deep representations reorganize rapidly near the grokking transition. Supporting result: Layerwise checkpoint trajectories connect a qualitative geometry change to generalization across three tasks, MLPs, transformers, and five-to-ten seeds per setting.
included	w_e93a08062ac7d030	How Transformers Learn Causal Structure with Gradient Descent	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	715	141	0	0	Title: How Transformers Learn Causal Structure with Gradient Descent. Claim: The transformer learns the latent causal graph as an attention computation. Supporting result: The graph is hidden from the model, the learned attention matrix is formally proved to converge to its adjacency through mutual-information gradients, and the forward-pass construction uses it for in-context transition estimation and OOD prediction. Claim: Gradient descent progressively aligns first-layer attention with a latent causal graph. Supporting result: Training dynamics, sequence-length, graph, and prior controls connect adjacency recovery to in-context prediction; end-to-end theory establishes the optimization-to-behavior link.
included	w_3addefc2ae588ee1	How Transformers Learn to Plan via Multi-Token Prediction	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	771	135	0	0	Title: How Transformers Learn to Plan via Multi-Token Prediction. Claim: Multi-token training learns a reverse-reasoning predecessor representation that is used for planning. Supporting result: The circuit is not supplied in the graph task, is formally derived from the gradient dynamics, and its learned attention computation retrieves predecessor relations that produce the planned path; next-token and objective controls do not form the same solution. Claim: Training objective and capacity determine whether a transformer develops a planning circuit. Supporting result: Multi-token versus next-token training, checkpoint, capacity, and data controls connect the formation of predecessor/content attention structure to successful reverse planning across repeated runs.
included	w_6fc4c4ab9c9916f7	Hysteresis, neural avalanches, and critical behavior near a first-order transition of a spiking neural network	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	872	183	0	0	Title: Hysteresis, neural avalanches, and critical behavior near a first-order transition of a spiking neural network. Claim: Crossing the pseudo-spinodal produces an abrupt first-order transition into collective pattern replay. Supporting result: The identified drive control produces a discontinuous firing-rate and spatiotemporal-order transition; reverse sweeps, multiple noise levels, three system sizes, finite-size avalanche collapse, and predicted exponent relations replicate the internal/behavioral boundary. Claim: External drive, noise, and size control a disordered-to-pattern-replay organization. Supporting result: Drive ramps, noise sweeps, and finite-size series connect the learned network's low-rate state, metastable alternation, and collective attractor replay to stored-pattern behavior, with hysteresis and avalanche controls delimiting the regimes.
included	w_86f10686a7226020	ILDR: Geometric Early Detection of Grokking	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	550	120	0	0	Title: ILDR: Geometric Early Detection of Grokking. Claim: Post-grokking representational variance drops by 1,696 times. Supporting result: A fixed held-out threshold detects a sharp inter/intra-class geometry transition; eight-seed replication and bidirectional interventions link it to behavior. Claim: Held-out representation geometry reorganizes before grokking. Supporting result: Checkpointed ILDR trajectories lead generalization on modular arithmetic and permutation composition across eight seeds, and optimizer interventions move the onset.
included	w_c053dda032e0c8cc	Is Random Attention Sufficient for Sequence Modeling? Disentangling Trainable Components in the Transformer	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	923	163	0	0	Title: Is Random Attention Sufficient for Sequence Modeling? Disentangling Trainable Components in the Transformer. Claim: Frozen-query/key transformers still learn input-dependent induction structure through their trainable representations. Supporting result: The induction pattern is absent from the random frozen attention weights, is formally expressible by the learned residual/MLP computation, and appears only in architectures that solve retrieval and language modeling; input-independent MixiT supplies the negative functional control. Claim: Depth controls representation collapse and the loss of sequence-modeling performance under random attention. Supporting result: Depth, width, head-count, and component-freezing series connect covariance collapse in random transformers to degraded tasks, while the signal-preserving MixiT control prevents collapse and separates memorization from input-dependent reasoning.
included	w_01e15d37549ba1fc	Latent Algorithmic Structure Precedes Grokking: A Mechanistic Study of ReLU MLPs on Modular Arithmetic	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	614	129	0	0	Title: Latent Algorithmic Structure Precedes Grokking: A Mechanistic Study of ReLU MLPs on Modular Arithmetic. Claim: ReLU MLPs learn near-binary square-wave and phase-sum structure. Supporting result: A formally extracted idealized model built from the learned frequencies and phases reaches 95.5% even when its source model does not grok, establishing functional use. Claim: The encoded algorithm sharpens over training before behavioral grokking. Supporting result: Checkpointed square-wave binarization and phase alignment precede and predict the later generalization transition, including noisy-data controls.
included	w_1414f43f732405e6	Learning through atypical phase transitions in overparameterized neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	903	173	0	0	Title: Learning through atypical phase transitions in overparameterized neural networks. Claim: A discontinuous local-entropy transition marks the appearance of accessible wide minima after interpolation. Supporting result: A formal order parameter identifies the distinct transition and predicts the algorithmic fitting boundary; independent samples, repeat runs, SGD/ADAM/message-passing controls, MNIST and CIFAR experiments pair the landscape transition with learning and generalization behavior. Claim: Overparameterization changes the solution landscape from inaccessible sharp minima to atypical wide regions used by efficient learners. Supporting result: Analytic parameter-to-sample sweeps and numerical binary, committee, multilayer, and convolutional models connect wide-minimum formation to trainability and improving generalization across algorithms, initializations, samples, and datasets.
included	w_9f65ba32c1a08d74	Low-Dimensional and Transversely Curved Optimization Dynamics in Grokking	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	719	133	0	0	Title: Low-Dimensional and Transversely Curved Optimization Dynamics in Grokking. Claim: A sharp transverse-curvature transition precedes the behavioral transition. Supporting result: The internal spectral and commutator transition is localized across 108 learning-rate runs and replicated tasks/seeds; random-subspace and dose-response projection interventions link it directly to grokking. Claim: Grokking follows a low-dimensional execution path whose transverse geometry changes during training. Supporting result: Training checkpoints and learning-rate/weight-decay controls connect a stable low-dimensional path and growing transverse curvature to delayed generalization across four operations and multiple seeds.
included	w_5cfae990f2e4ea0e	Multiple-Timescale Neural Networks: Generation of History-Dependent Sequences and Inference Through Autonomous Bifurcations	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	947	181	0	0	Title: Multiple-Timescale Neural Networks: Generation of History-Dependent Sequences and Inference Through Autonomous Bifurcations. Claim: Local learning shapes slow-fast feedback into history-dependent sequential attractor dynamics. Supporting result: Although the two-timescale architecture is prescribed, the recurrent relationship is learned rather than embedded; fixed-point and basin analyses characterize its autonomous bifurcations, and noise/perturbation tests plus a novel-sequence concatenation control across independent realizations show functional recall and inference. Claim: The relation between slow-neural and synaptic-learning timescales controls the learned recall regime. Supporting result: A timescale-ratio sweep over fifty network realizations links a shared performance optimum to three distinct learned fixed-point organizations: sequential coexistence, skipped targets, or domination by the most recently learned target.
included	w_c31a237682405c3b	Noise-Driven Escape from Metastable Phases explains Grokking in Deep Neural Networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	562	121	0	0	Title: Noise-Driven Escape from Metastable Phases explains Grokking in Deep Neural Networks. Claim: First-order L2 transitions create metastable feature states. Supporting result: Analytic barriers, deliberate trapping, 50-seed experiments, and an Arrhenius fit (R²=0.991) link abrupt rank escape to the behavioral transition. Claim: SGD noise drives escape between learned-feature ranks over training. Supporting result: Learning rate, batch size, regularization, and time jointly control internal rank and test error; escape times span two orders of magnitude.
included	w_0308d55890a5b9c6	On the Emergence of Induction Heads for In-Context Learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	599	110	0	0	Title: On the Emergence of Induction Heads for In-Context Learning. Claim: Low loss coincides with an induction-head phase transition. Supporting result: The internal parameters and ICL behavior transition together; restricting training to the three-dimensional mechanism leaves emergence unaffected, and asymptotic theory predicts its threshold. Claim: Induction-head emergence time scales quadratically with context length. Supporting result: Training trajectories and theory track the ordered emergence of three internal parameters and the accompanying ICL loss transition across context lengths.
included	w_25ba5099b834e43a	On the Mechanism and Dynamics of Modular Addition: Fourier Features, Lottery Ticket, and Grokking	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	905	158	0	0	Title: On the Mechanism and Dynamics of Modular Addition: Fourier Features, Lottery Ticket, and Grokking. Claim: Weight decay refines the network into a sparse Fourier representation. Supporting result: Single-frequency neurons and diversified phase-symmetric features cancel noise; activation and diversification ablations preserve or destroy perfect modular-addition accuracy as predicted. Claim: Randomly initialized neurons learn single-frequency Fourier features. Supporting result: The learned phase/frequency basis is derived formally from gradient dynamics and shown experimentally to implement the majority-vote modular-addition algorithm. Claim: Training passes through memorization, feature learning, and cleanup phases. Supporting result: Checkpointed Fourier coefficients, phase alignment, norms, and accuracy jointly trace the internal algorithm from random initialization to generalization.
included	w_6ffbda57ab6a27ae	Optimal reinforcement learning near the edge of a synchronization transition	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	850	165	0	0	Title: Optimal reinforcement learning near the edge of a synchronization transition. Claim: The learned spiking network crosses a sharp synchronization boundary where task performance is optimized. Supporting result: The delay-controlled internal transition shifts with size and is independently confirmed by subcritical, scale-free, and supercritical avalanche distributions; twenty-realization ensembles, untrained controls, four task complexities, and multiple sizes pair the boundary with behavior. Claim: Axonal delay and network size control synchronization organization and reinforcement-learning performance. Supporting result: Delay, size, task-complexity, connectivity, plasticity, and STDP-parameter series connect the synchronized-to-asynchronous organization to stimulus-response accuracy across ensembles of twenty network realizations.
included	w_dff3d1da5e63f8e0	Phase Transitions in Attention: A Bayesian Theory of Copy Head Emergence	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	571	109	0	0	Title: Phase Transitions in Attention: A Bayesian Theory of Copy Head Emergence. Claim: Softmax attention exhibits a first-order phase transition. Supporting result: A low-dimensional order parameter crosses an analytic data threshold; Bayesian sampling, Adam training, and linear-attention controls reproduce the internal and behavioral regimes. Claim: The copy subcircuit changes qualitatively with training-data scale. Supporting result: Closed-form posterior predictions and Adam-trained networks connect data amount to structured attention and copy-task performance.
included	w_e290bc51cfc6cc89	Phase transitions reveal hierarchical structure in deep neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	592	108	0	0	Title: Phase transitions reveal hierarchical structure in deep neural networks. Claim: Each transition is governed by a critical saddle point. Supporting result: Analytic Hessian conditions and repeated Pathfinder trajectories locate discontinuous basin changes, per-digit behavior changes, and mode-connectivity controls at the same boundaries. Claim: Regularization traces models through distinct hierarchical accuracy basins. Supporting result: Varying L2 strength reorganizes the loss-landscape Hessian and the digit classes encoded by trained models, with corresponding accuracy changes.
included	w_b19041cd869bacfa	Post-Grokking Collapse at the Representation-Readout Interface in Muon-Trained Transformers	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	635	126	0	0	Title: Post-Grokking Collapse at the Representation-Readout Interface in Muon-Trained Transformers. Claim: The isolated task-aligned Fourier family remains sufficient during masking failure. Supporting result: Fourier filtering retains perfect accuracy in the reduced family at 43 checkpoints; removing the adversarial remainder or rescaling the family restores up to 99.9%. Claim: Representation-readout dynamics cause post-grokking failure over training. Supporting result: Checkpoint and bit-identical freeze experiments across configurations and five seeds causally connect optimizer-driven interface motion to lost generalization.
included	w_85b812c5c78e647c	Predicting the Emergence of Induction Heads in Language Model Pretraining	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	565	104	0	0	Title: Predicting the Emergence of Induction Heads in Language Model Pretraining. Claim: Induction-head metrics and associative recall change abruptly together. Supporting result: Controlled repetition-frequency and reliability sweeps produce a stable formation boundary across natural and synthetic environments, with matched behavioral transition. Claim: Batch size and context size predict when induction heads form. Supporting result: Natural, semi-natural, and synthetic sweeps link data exposure and internal head formation to associative-recall/ICL behavior.
included	w_d27b97e00fa37f8b	Prediction horizon shapes representations in predictive learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1046	196	0	0	Title: Prediction horizon shapes representations in predictive learning. Claim: Long-horizon prediction learns a low-dimensional state representation that preserves predictive structure. Supporting result: Participation ratio, effective-rank, rank-truncation, and held-out-pair tests show selective concentration into state/action directions sufficient for prediction and associated with better OOD accuracy. Claim: Predictive learning spontaneously recovers the environment's latent manifold. Supporting result: One-hot observations remove prior neighborhood geometry and no manifold regularizer is used; formal OLS/implicit-bias analysis, nonlinear controls, rank truncation, and held-out behavior connect the learned geometry to prediction. Claim: Prediction horizon and depth control recovery of latent task geometry. Supporting result: Horizon, environment size, depth, architecture, and dataset series reorganize hidden states from unstructured solutions to latent manifolds while improving compositional and out-of-distribution prediction.
included	w_0c85822618800428	Predictive learning as a network mechanism for extracting low-dimensional latent space representations	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	927	161	0	0	Title: Predictive learning as a network mechanism for extracting low-dimensional latent space representations. Claim: Predictive learning extracts a low-dimensional manifold from high-dimensional observations. Supporting result: The representation selectively retains latent position/action variables while discarding sensor color; prediction and 12 non-predictive/noise controls establish functional value. Claim: The learned representation inherits the latent environment's group geometry. Supporting result: Without latent-coordinate supervision, recurrent dynamics form a formally characterized group representation used to transform actions into future predictions. Claim: Latent-space signal transfers into leading representation modes during learning. Supporting result: Training trajectories link predictive error to a qualitative manifold reorganization in progressively harder tasks, including 50-network comparisons.
included	w_cf357442f5aa7893	Predictive pursuit emerges in high-dimensional recurrent neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	761	138	0	0	Title: Predictive pursuit emerges in high-dimensional recurrent neural networks. Claim: Pursuit training produces egocentric target coordinates despite receiving no egocentric target input. Supporting result: The learned coordinate-like tuning is formally quantified, distinguishes current from prospective states, and target-unit versus random-unit ablations selectively impair pursuit, establishing use by the recurrent controller. Claim: Reservoir rank controls the appearance of allocentric prospective pursuit representations. Supporting result: Rank sweeps from ten to one thousand connect high-dimensional recurrent organization to predictive target trajectories and pursuit performance across thirty networks, with objective, readout, and unit controls.
included	w_e1dd76d318faa330	Predictive sequence learning in the hippocampal formation	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1327	233	0	0	Title: Predictive sequence learning in the hippocampal formation. Claim: Temporal prediction compresses redundant sensory inputs into localized sequential representations. Supporting result: Across ten localization networks, predictive hidden states carry more position information than high-dimensional inputs and current-loss controls; for MNIST sequences they retain class/sequence structure while generating the next images and completing withheld suffixes, which non-predictive controls do not. Claim: Sequence training produces a sparse Toeplitz recurrent organization with line-attractor dynamics. Supporting result: Without a prescribed recurrent connectivity pattern, training yields a formally characterized near-Toeplitz/Mexican-hat organization; the recurrent state itself generates ordered replay from noise and completes partial sequences, linking the learned manifold to model behavior. Claim: Error-coded CA1 responses appear rapidly while the CA3 internal representation develops over repeated experience. Supporting result: Training exposure after an environment switch moves the model from immediate CA1 prediction-error coding to a learned CA3 sequence representation; the resulting recurrent organization generates replay and pattern completion, with familiar/noisy-environment and non-predictive controls.
included	w_f3172e652cebe4b6	Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1657	308	0	0	Title: Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability. Claim: The generalizing computation compresses modular addition into a small set of Fourier directions and a sparse circuit. Supporting result: Five key frequencies explain 95% of logit variance; ten sine/cosine directions support low loss; ablating a key frequency destroys performance while ablating the other roughly 95% of frequencies does not and can improve loss. Claim: Grokking is a transition between memorizing and generalizing organizational regimes linked to a rapid behavioral change. Supporting result: At a distinct training interval, test loss drops while Fourier-component sparsity rises sharply during cleanup; the corresponding trajectories are reproduced for four additional random seeds and checked under architecture, modulus, and regularization variations. Claim: The transformer learns a Fourier/trigonometric coordinate system for modular addition that was not prescribed by the architecture. Supporting result: Weights and activations implement addition as rotations in learned frequency coordinates; the structure is mathematically reconstructed and causally tested by Fourier-space ablations, and generalizing runs learn variants of the same algorithm. Claim: Training progression produces a qualitative reorganization from memorization to a sparse Fourier circuit and then cleanup, enabling generalization. Supporting result: Training epoch is logged against restricted/excluded loss, Fourier sparsity, and behavior; the circuit-formation and cleanup phases reorganize the learned computation and precede or accompany test generalization across five seeds.
included	w_248da8719a3b92f1	Quantum-data-driven dynamical transition in quantum learning	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	740	143	0	0	Title: Quantum-data-driven dynamical transition in quantum learning. Claim: Crossing a target-state boundary produces a dynamical bifurcation in the learned QNTK. Supporting result: Fixed-point stability and topological charge identify the internal transition and predict the convergence-class change; perturbation analyses, numerical tests, and twelve device rounds reproduce the paired kernel/error dynamics. Claim: Quantum target constraints control a qualitative change in learned kernel and error dynamics. Supporting result: Target-value, data-count, and depth conditions separate fixed-point classes of the learned QNTK and pair them with exponential or polynomial training convergence in theory, simulation, and quantum-device runs.
included	w_34505df745b929e9	Random noise promotes slow heterogeneous synaptic dynamics important for robust working memory computation	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1026	204	0	0	Title: Random noise promotes slow heterogeneous synaptic dynamics important for robust working memory computation. Claim: Noise training creates a cell-type-specific slow inhibitory eigenmode organization used to maintain stimulus information. Supporting result: Although per-unit time constants are learnable parameters, preferential inhibitory specialization is not imposed. Spectral stability, stimulus-selective activity, fixed-excitatory-time-constant, and task controls connect the learned dominant modes to persistent working-memory behavior. Claim: Training-noise level controls a qualitative specialization of inhibitory dynamics tied to working-memory robustness. Supporting result: Six noise-channel levels across fifty random initializations shift learned inhibitory decay constants and dominant eigenmodes toward slower near-boundary dynamics while changing training success, long-delay accuracy, and perturbation resilience; no-noise, fixed-parameter, cell-ratio, and non-memory-task controls delimit the effect.
included	w_71088ab66e735f6d	Recurrent Neural Networks Learn to Store and Generate Sequences using Non-Linear Representations	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	762	136	0	0	Title: Recurrent Neural Networks Learn to Store and Generate Sequences using Non-Linear Representations. Claim: Narrow recurrent networks learn an onion-like scaled-token coordinate system for sequence memory. Supporting result: The nonlinear code is not prescribed, is mathematically characterized across token positions, and interventions that preserve its structure recover about ninety percent of autoregressive decoding while destructive controls impair behavior. Claim: Network width changes the sequence representation from nonlinear onion storage to a linear code. Supporting result: Widths from 48 to 1024 and training trajectories connect the internal-code switch to sequence reconstruction, with five-run replications and representation interventions.
included	w_67833e8bc8bb92f5	Redundant representations help generalization in wide neural networks * , †	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1328	251	0	0	Title: Redundant representations help generalization in wide neural networks * , †. Claim: A small random chunk of the learned final representation faithfully preserves the task information in the full layer. Supporting result: Without retraining, sufficiently large neuron subsets retain near-zero training error and the full network's test scaling, linearly reconstruct nearly all full-layer variance, and leave only weakly correlated residuals, directly demonstrating a smaller functional representation. Claim: Representation redundancy appears only beyond a critical interpolation-linked width. Supporting result: The internal redundancy onset is paired with the zero-training-error boundary and a change to inverse-square-root generalization scaling; repeated random chunks, multiple architectures and datasets, plus regularization and training-duration controls reproduce and move the boundary. Claim: Increasing width beyond interpolation reorganizes the final representation into redundant task-feature copies. Supporting result: Width series across fully connected, DenseNet, Wide-ResNet, and ResNet models link the onset and increasing number of redundant chunks to zero training error and continued test-error improvement; architecture, early-stopping, and weight-decay controls remove the organization as predicted.
included	w_de78b34865a33cc1	Sequential predictive learning is a unifying theory for hippocampal representation and replay	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	876	163	0	0	Title: Sequential predictive learning is a unifying theory for hippocampal representation and replay. Claim: Sequential prediction learns a continuous attractor reflecting environmental geometry. Supporting result: From spatially ambiguous egocentric images and without assigned place coordinates, the recurrent system learns a formally measured attractive manifold that generates coherent sensory-associated replay, recent-location recall, and head-direction-guided novel trajectories. Claim: At least two predicted timesteps are required for an attractive cognitive map and replay. Supporting result: Prediction-horizon sweeps from zero to six steps pair the onset of a continuous attractor with coherent, plausible offline trajectories; nine-seed, hyperparameter, action-code, architecture, geometry, ambiguity, and continuous-environment controls reproduce the transition.
included	w_38e23f5995c71d9b	Spatial reasoning via recurrent neural dynamics in mouse retrosplenial cortex	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1381	241	0	0	Title: Spatial reasoning via recurrent neural dynamics in mouse retrosplenial cortex. Claim: A roughly three-dimensional recurrent manifold retains the variables needed for spatial hypothesis updating. Supporting result: Correlation dimension, stable pairwise dynamics, and a 128-unit versus random-network control establish a compact state; the manifold retains landmark, displacement, and hypothesis information, performs near-Bayesian localization, and attracts large perturbations back to functional trajectories. Claim: The recurrent network learns multimodal hypothesis states and a displacement-to-location coordinate transformation. Supporting result: Landmark identities and hypothesis coordinates are not provided. Correlation dimension and attractor trajectories formally characterize the learned states, and controls that reveal landmark identity remove the bimodal code while the original dynamics maintain, update, and use it for choices. Claim: Navigation learning produces a low-dimensional recurrent hypothesis organization paired with successful reasoning. Supporting result: Low-performing training sessions and matched random-recurrent controls lack the acquired organization, while trained networks and learned animals develop hypothesis states that predict correct choices; architectures, environments, initializations, and animals replicate the relationship.
included	w_b1a722c7add3a18c	Specialization of softmax attention heads: insights from the high-dimensional single-location model	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	751	136	0	0	Title: Specialization of softmax attention heads: insights from the high-dimensional single-location model. Claim: Heads specialize rapidly when optimization escapes a symmetric saddle. Supporting result: The internal order parameters and prediction loss change together at saddle escape, with one hundred independent runs, high-dimensional theory, finite-model simulations, and an image-data control replicating the transition. Claim: Training time reorganizes attention heads from a shared average into sequentially specialized predictors. Supporting result: Gradient-flow theory and training trajectories connect head order parameters to loss reduction; dimensional, signal, initialization, and MNIST controls reproduce the specialization sequence.
included	w_a124ea491b1e38a3	Spectral Entropy Collapse as a Phase Transition in Delayed Generalisation: An Interventional and Predictive Framework for Grokkin	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	655	119	0	0	Title: Spectral Entropy Collapse as a Phase Transition in Delayed Generalisation: An Interventional and Predictive Framework for Grokkin. Claim: Representations concentrate into task-structured directions. Supporting result: Entropy reduction retains Fourier task structure; norm-matched representation mixing delays both the reduction and grokking, while MLP controls delimit sufficiency. Claim: Spectral entropy collapse precedes delayed generalisation during training. Supporting result: Across training checkpoints, tasks, architectures, and seeds, task-aligned spectral concentration develops before accuracy; mixing interventions shift both timings.
included	w_8c5442dfb760aae0	Spectral phase transitions and trainability in neural network learning dynamics	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	591	113	0	0	Title: Spectral phase transitions and trainability in neural network learning dynamics. Claim: A dynamical BBP transition occurs at a critical time and step size. Supporting result: The exact teacher-student boundary predicts eigenvalue detachment and performance regimes; nonlinear, multilayer, and stochastic simulations serve as independent controls. Claim: An isolated signal eigenvalue emerges from the random bulk during training. Supporting result: Time, step size, variance, and sample-load sweeps connect spectral signal alignment to trainability in analytic and nonlinear networks.
included	w_4ec0813caf380920	Structure-Specific Representational Priors Causally Control the Grokking Delay	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	651	119	0	0	Title: Structure-Specific Representational Priors Causally Control the Grokking Delay. Claim: Generalization requires concentration into the task's periodic feature family. Supporting result: Top-frequency concentration and held-out class clustering are selectively retained in successful runs; structurally matched interventions and norm controls establish functional relevance. Claim: The delay is the time required to form task-structured representations. Supporting result: Representation probes evolve through training and predict generalization in all 95 runs; true, sibling, shuffled, and exact norm-replay interventions alter the trajectories.
included	w_5c54f66764cf4f97	Temporal hierarchy in spiking neural networks	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	731	134	0	0	Title: Temporal hierarchy in spiking neural networks. Claim: Homogeneously initialized spiking networks spontaneously form a layerwise temporal hierarchy. Supporting result: Individual time constants are learned without a prescribed hierarchy, are formally summarized by layer and depth, and imposed, reversed, frozen, and shuffled controls show that the resulting organization supports multiscale temporal computation. Claim: Task hierarchy and network depth control a layerwise hierarchy of learned neuronal time constants. Supporting result: Temporal-amplitude, depth, imposed/reversed-hierarchy, and task controls connect the learned time-constant allocation to temporal XOR and speech-event performance across repeated trials.
included	w_f05eeedb3d84fbb7	The Geometry of Multi-Task Grokking: Transverse Instability, Superposition, and Weight Decay Phase Structure	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	693	129	0	0	Title: The Geometry of Multi-Task Grokking: Transverse Instability, Superposition, and Weight Decay Phase Structure. Claim: A transverse-instability transition separates the memorizing and generalizing regimes. Supporting result: The rapid internal transition is replicated across modular operations and seeds; deleting orthogonal gradient components prevents the matched generalization transition while norm-matched controls do not. Claim: Weight decay controls qualitative internal regimes in multi-task grokking. Supporting result: Checkpointed attention spectra and trajectory geometry vary across weight-decay, task, and seed controls in step with the memorization/generalization regimes.
included	w_e0964d9cc077c1f7	The Kinetics of Training: A Driven-Nucleation Rate Law for Emergence, Plasticity Loss, and Circuit Control in Language Models	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	650	129	0	0	Title: The Kinetics of Training: A Driven-Nucleation Rate Law for Emergence, Plasticity Loss, and Circuit Control in Language Models. Claim: A capability ignites when a no-partial-credit circuit alignment barrier is crossed. Supporting result: Preregistered predictions, held-out models, 32 ablation cells, scheduled ignition, and query-key reinitialization establish an abrupt circuit/capability boundary. Claim: Capability arrival depends on missing circuit parts across training and scale. Supporting result: Pythia checkpoints across seven capabilities and three scales connect circuit-part alignment, precursor strength, and behavioral ignition.
included	w_4ae2e575bb61224a	The mechanistic basis of data dependence and abrupt learning in an in-context classification task	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	591	111	0	0	Title: The mechanistic basis of data dependence and abrupt learning in an in-context classification task. Claim: ICL is driven by the abrupt emergence of an induction head. Supporting result: Six-seed curves show reproducible circuit and accuracy jumps; parameter ablations and a predictive phenomenological model rule out alternative operation sequences. Claim: An induction head forms during training and drives ICL. Supporting result: Checkpoint progress measures in the full and minimal networks track circuit construction and behavior across six seeds and controlled data distributions.
included	w_97808c92883566a2	Thinking Sparks!: Emergent Attention Heads in Reasoning Models During Post Training	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	637	116	0	0	Title: Thinking Sparks!: Emergent Attention Heads in Reasoning Models During Post Training. Claim: Post-training creates functionally specialized attention-head circuits for reasoning. Supporting result: The head roles are learned rather than assigned, are characterized by task-specific activation and information flow, and causal head ablation/scaling changes benchmark reasoning behavior. Claim: Specialized reasoning heads appear and are pruned across post-training stages. Supporting result: Post-training checkpoints, reward regimes, and model-family controls connect head recruitment and survival to reasoning accuracy and reward.
included	w_5fc248e95ef96553	Towards Empirical Interpretation of Internal Circuits and Properties in Grokked Transformers on Modular Polynomials	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	614	114	0	0	Title: Towards Empirical Interpretation of Internal Circuits and Properties in Grokked Transformers on Modular Polynomials. Claim: Grokked embeddings use sparse, operation-specific Fourier components. Supporting result: Restricted/ablated Fourier losses and pre-grokked transfer tests show when the reduced significant-frequency representation preserves or disrupts task performance. Claim: Fourier progress measures decrease with test-accuracy improvement. Supporting result: Three-seed training trajectories across arithmetic and polynomial tasks link internal Fourier reorganization to grokking and co-grokking.
included	w_29ea95f17f21c7ac	Transformers for dynamical systems learn transfer operators in-context	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	741	138	0	0	Title: Transformers for dynamical systems learn transfer operators in-context. Claim: The same learned dynamical-system mechanism transfers zero-shot between distinct systems. Supporting result: Models trained on one system are evaluated on qualitatively different systems across hundreds of train/test pairs; delay-embedding, Markov, and parroting controls plus operator analyses document reuse of the same mechanism. Claim: A transformer learns delay-coordinate state reconstruction and a transfer-operator computation. Supporting result: Neither latent state coordinates nor the operator are supplied; the learned attention computation is formally matched to delay embedding and transfer operators and predicts future dynamics in context.
included	w_7478c4587d99ea18	Unveiling Transformers with LEGO: a synthetic reasoning task	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1155	209	0	0	Title: Unveiling Transformers with LEGO: a synthetic reasoning task. Claim: An attention mechanism learned during unrelated language pretraining is reused for the LEGO reasoning family. Supporting result: The same association pattern is documented before and after transfer from natural-language pretraining, randomized-token and shortcut-resistant task variants rule out lexical memorization, and three-run BERT/ALBERT tests replicate longer-chain generalization. Claim: Transformers learn an identical-token association structure used to propagate equality constraints. Supporting result: The attention pattern is learned rather than supplied, is explicitly characterized at head and layer level, and architectural imitation plus removal/replacement controls establish its functional role in multi-step reasoning. Claim: Pretraining, depth, and sequence length control the formation and routing of reasoning-relevant attention structure. Supporting result: Random initialization, pretrained BERT/ALBERT, depth, and data controls connect association/manipulation heads and information-flow order to length-generalization behavior across independent runs.
included	w_f5f5600670f31041	Weight Decay Regimes in Grokking Transformers: Cheap Online Diagnostics	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	704	130	0	0	Title: Weight Decay Regimes in Grokking Transformers: Cheap Online Diagnostics. Claim: A localized weight-decay threshold separates internal and behavioral phases. Supporting result: Bootstrap and jackknife threshold estimates use hundreds of runs; multi-task and cross-architecture replications plus paired head reinitialization and norm-clipping controls isolate attention-pattern structure at the transition. Claim: Weight decay controls memorization, developmental, and collapse regimes in attention organization. Supporting result: Dense weight-decay, model-scale, task, architecture, and long-horizon series pair attention coordination/differentiation with the corresponding generalization regimes.
included	w_7afa59979906b85f	What Needs to Go Right for an Induction Head? A Mechanistic Study of In-Context Learning Circuits and Their Formation	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1259	240	0	0	Title: What Needs to Go Right for an Induction Head? A Mechanistic Study of In-Context Learning Circuits and Their Formation. Claim: Interacting induction subcircuits cause a sharp training transition from chance-level behavior to near-perfect in-context generalization. Supporting result: Loss and accuracy change sharply at the same training interval that induction strength rises. Clamping the match/copy components throughout training sharpens, advances, or removes the phase profile, causally showing how three smoothly learned subcircuits generate the discontinuity. Data controls shift the transition and the main mechanism replicates over five model initializations. Claim: Training progression and controlled increases in task complexity reorganize the induction circuit and shift when in-context learning forms. Supporting result: Sequences seen is an explicit training control variable: a loss plateau gives way to near-perfect test accuracy while induction heads form. Increasing class count or label count delays distinct subcircuits and the full transition, and training-time clamping isolates the mechanisms producing those shifts. Central subcircuit results hold across five initialization seeds and scaling trends reproduce across most seeds.
included	w_33b529766cc60dd2	What One Cannot, Two Can: Two-Layer Transformers Provably Represent Induction Heads on Any-Order Markov Chains	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	880	175	0	0	Title: What One Cannot, Two Can: Two-Layer Transformers Provably Represent Induction Heads on Any-Order Markov Chains. Claim: A two-layer, one-head-per-layer transformer compactly represents any conditional k-gram through a dyadic context code and matching operation. Supporting result: The constructive proof encodes the length-k context in the first layer and matches it in the second with one head per layer and compact parameter/precision bounds, avoiding the exponential-width one-layer alternative. Claim: Increasing depth from one to two layers creates a qualitatively new internal organization that efficiently implements higher-order induction. Supporting result: Depth is the explicit variable: one layer requires exponential width, while two single-head layers implement distinct context-encoding and context-matching stages and recover the conditional k-gram behavior.
included	w_5362be8fefcfa6c2	When and Where: A Model Hippocampal Network Unifies Formation of Time Cells and Place Cells	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	1034	199	0	0	Title: When and Where: A Model Hippocampal Network Unifies Formation of Time Cells and Place Cells. Claim: Masked reconstruction learns distinct place-cell-like and time-cell-like recurrent representations. Supporting result: Uniformly initialized recurrence develops formally measured spatial or sequential temporal codes without those hidden coordinates being assigned; the recurrent state predicts fully masked intervals, and recurrent-matrix replacement plus input/output randomization isolates the learned recurrent regime as the source of sequential coding while reconstruction readout establishes functional use. Claim: Changing the balance and availability of spatial and temporal inputs reorganizes the hidden representation. Supporting result: Event-duration, spatial-mask, and fixed-dimension channel-ratio series move the same predictive autoencoder between place-like, mixed, and time-like population regimes while it reconstructs withheld sensory trajectories; pure spatial and temporal tasks serve as endpoint controls.
included	w_b1b2841abfaf8ef2	Which Attention Heads Matter for In-Context Learning?	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	886	181	0	0	Title: Which Attention Heads Matter for In-Context Learning?. Claim: A small set of function-vector heads contains a compact task representation sufficient to recover much of ICL behavior. Supporting result: Preserving the top 2% FV heads preserves ICL accuracy under broader ablation; summing selected task-conditioned head outputs into one function vector and injecting it into shuffled prompts recovers most clean-run accuracy for all but Pythia 2.8B. Claim: Increasing model size and training time reorganizes important ICL heads from induction-like heads toward function-vector heads, with the latter increasingly supporting few-shot ICL. Supporting result: Model size and training checkpoint are explicit variables; FV-head ablation becomes more behaviorally consequential in larger models, and tracked individual heads lose induction score while gaining FV score during training.
included	w_9f42a69c53e47f06	Why the Brain Consolidates: Predictive Forgetting for Optimal Generalisation	INCLUDE	supported-claims-results	744	142	0	0	Title: Why the Brain Consolidates: Predictive Forgetting for Optimal Generalisation. Claim: Learned refinement selectively removes input information while retaining task-relevant information. Supporting result: Mutual-information estimates, held-out cross-fitting, regularization controls, and replay tests show a reduced representation with preserved or improved prediction rather than indiscriminate collapse. Claim: Iterative refinement depth and capacity control progressive predictive forgetting and generalization. Supporting result: Refinement-step, model, objective, and dataset series show nuisance information falling while label information and test performance improve across five vision datasets, GSM8K, and fifty-seed simulations.
audited	w_7ced2a0538a6bf26	A Bayesian Perspective on the Role of Epistemic Uncertainty for Delayed Generalization in In-Context Learning	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	2123	430	0	0	Title: A Bayesian Perspective on the Role of Epistemic Uncertainty for Delayed Generalization in In-Context Learning. Claim: In-context learning enables transformers to adapt to new tasks from a few examples at inference time, while grokking highlights that this generalization can emerge… Supporting result: A Bayesian Perspective on the Role of Epistemic Uncertainty for Delayed Generalization in In-Context Learning Abdessamed Qchohi abdessamed.qchohi@eurecom.fr EURECOM, France Simone Rossi simone.rossi@eurecom.fr EURECOM, France Abstract In-context learning enables transformers to adapt to new tasks from a few examples at inference time, while grokking highlights that this general- ization can emerge abruptly only after prolonged training. Claim: In-context learning enables transformers to adapt to new tasks from a few examples at inference time, while grokking highlights that this generalization can emerge… Supporting result: Additionally, we provide theoretical support with a simplified Bayesian linear model, showing that asymptotically both delayed generalization and uncertainty peaks arise from the same underlying spectral mechanism, which links grokking time to uncertainty dynamics. 1 Introduction In-Context Learning (ICL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm in which Large Language Models (LLMs) can learn to perform new tasks by conditioning on a few examples provided in the input prompt (Brown et al., 2020). Claim: In-context learning enables transformers to adapt to new tasks from a few examples at inference time, while grokking highlights that this generalization can emerge… Supporting result: Additionally, we provide theoretical support with a simplified Bayesian linear model, showing that asymptotically both delayed generalization and uncertainty peaks arise from the same underlying spectral mechanism, which links grokking time to uncertainty dynamics. 1 Introduction In-Context Learning (ICL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm in which Large Language Models (LLMs) can learn to perform new tasks by conditioning on a few examples provided in the input prompt (Brown et al., 2020).
