audited	w_c05624cc0be4c2cb	A jamming transition from under- to over-parametrization affects generalization in deep learning	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	717	150	0	0	Title: A jamming transition from under- to over-parametrization affects generalization in deep learning. Claim: The number of unsatisfied constraints jumps discontinuously at the jamming line. Supporting result: The internal discontinuity and behavioral cusp are reproduced across sample sizes, depths, losses, activations, and random versus structured data; early stopping removes the cusp as predicted. Claim: Width and sample count control a qualitative loss-landscape regime change tied to generalization. Supporting result: Systematic parameter/sample sweeps connect the satisfiable/unsatisfiable constraint organization and Hessian geometry to training fit and a cusp in test error across random data and MNIST.
audited	w_f94f564014be4c25	A Mechanistic Analysis of Transformers for Dynamical Systems	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	759	147	0	0	Title: A Mechanistic Analysis of Transformers for Dynamical Systems. Claim: A three-dimensional latent state learned from scalar histories preserves the system's predictive dynamics. Supporting result: The compact latent supports held-out forecasting and reconstruction of the higher-dimensional flow field; context and latent-dimension ablations plus five-fold cross-validation establish faithful functional retention. Claim: Context length and latent dimension control recovery of a predictive dynamical-system state. Supporting result: Context, latent-width, parameter-conditioning, and architecture controls connect the change from folded to separated latent geometry to forecasting and flow-field reconstruction over ten seeds and cross-validation folds.
audited	w_3f7299052e18118d	A Neural Network Model of Free Recall Learns Multiple Memory Strategies	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1325	252	0	0	Title: A Neural Network Model of Free Recall Learns Multiple Memory Strategies. Claim: The best free-recall models compress list content into a stimulus-invariant serial-index scaffold that supports efficient and robust retrieval. Supporting result: Index explains substantially more hidden-state variance for the memory-palace strategy, cross-decodes across items and study/response phases, and the same recurrent state is the cosine-similarity cue used to retrieve stored memories; the strategy has the best accuracy and noise robustness. Claim: The learned index abstraction generalizes across unseen item sets, phases, and recall tasks. Supporting result: Eight-to-64-item cross-decoding and study-to-response decoding test invariance inside the same free-recall setup; conditional-recall networks are trained separately and adopt task-specialized strategies. Claim: The network learns an item-independent serial-position coordinate system analogous to a memory palace, without a prescribed recall order. Supporting result: Across trained populations, a stable positional trajectory distinct from temporal-context strategies emerges despite reward being indifferent to recall order; it is reinstated during retrieval, linked to forward recall, and its incidence shifts predictably with discount and memory-flush controls.
audited	w_07d5eb02f4e65497	A phase transition between positional and semantic learning in a solvable model of dot-product attention	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	838	150	0	0	Title: A phase transition between positional and semantic learning in a solvable model of dot-product attention. Claim: A phase boundary separates positional and semantic attention regimes. Supporting result: At the predicted critical sample load, the positional order parameter gives way to semantic alignment while generalization crosses the positional-baseline boundary; analytic loss-landscape results and repeated finite-size simulations locate the same transition. Claim: Increasing sample complexity shifts attention from positional to semantic solutions. Supporting result: Sweeps over sample load alpha connect a switch in the semantic and positional order parameters to the validation-MSE advantage of the semantic solution; 24 problem instances, 30-run finite-size tests, and matrix/architecture controls reproduce the regimes.
