audited	w_310671f6b2320a54	Are Your Reasoning Models Reasoning or Guessing? A Mechanistic Analysis of Hierarchical Reasoning Models	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1300	301	0	0	"Title: Are Your Reasoning Models Reasoning or Guessing? A Mechanistic Analysis of Hierarchical Reasoning Models. Claim: (b) ""Grokking"" dynamics in reasoning steps, i.e., the answer is not improved uniformly, but instead there is a critical reasoning step that suddenly makes… Supporting result: (b) “Grokking” dynamics in reasoning steps, i.e., the answer is not improved uniformly, but instead there is acritical reasoning stepthat suddenly makes the answer correct; (c)Existence of multi- ple fixed points. Claim: Leveraging this ""guessing"" picture, we propose three strategies to scale HRM's guesses: data augmentation (scaling the quality of guesses), input perturbation (scaling the number… Supporting result: We hypothesize that the recursion (outermost loop) of HRM serves as a way of scaling “guessing” attempts for a plausible latent state, chal- lenging the common belief that recursive reasoning boosts performance by incremental refinement. • Reasoning “gets lost” in the latent space, due to multi-stability of the reasoning landscape (Section 5): Closely inspecting the reasoning trajectory in the latent space, we are able to classify reasoning modes of HRM, among which the most interesting failure mode is when it “gets lost”, i.e., lingering around some misleading attractive point."
audited	w_8686cbb9af9f8fa5	Arithmetic Pedagogy for Language Models	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	500	88	0	0	Title: Arithmetic Pedagogy for Language Models. Claim: We investigate whether methods of human mathematics pedagogy can guide the training of language models toward arithmetic reasoning. Supporting result: Through large-scale training, Transformer models appear to form internal representations that approximate part of the structural regularity in mathematical relationships by capturing statistical regularities associated with operations, numerical relations, and problem-solving patterns [3, 11].
audited	w_8bf24f4dbe9ac287	Arithmetic with language models: From memorization to computation	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	603	150	0	0	Title: Arithmetic with language models: From memorization to computation. Claim: This work investigates how a language model, trained to predict the next token, can perform arithmetic computations generalizing beyond training data. Supporting result: More formally, given two integers 𝐴, 𝐵 (both in the range [0,127]) our input sequence (or prompt) is a 14-token string taking the form: 𝑎0 𝑎1 𝑎2 𝑎3 𝑎4 𝑎5 𝑎6 〈𝑜𝑝〉 𝑏0 𝑏1 𝑏2 𝑏3 𝑏4 𝑏5 𝑏6 where 𝑎𝑖, 𝑏𝑖 ∈ {‘0’, ‘1’} are the symbols corresponding to bits in 𝑖-th position in the binary representation of 𝐴 and 𝐵 respectively, and 〈𝑜𝑝〉 can be either ‘+’ or ‘×’.
audited	w_92e78fbf258ed40a	Assign and Add: A Mechanistic Study of Compositional Arithmetic	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1106	190	0	0	Title: Assign and Add: A Mechanistic Study of Compositional Arithmetic. Claim: In this paper, we study a mechanism for compositional generalization in transformers by considering a simple controlled setting involving variable assignment and modular addition. Supporting result: In this paper, we study a mechanism for compositional generalization in transformers by considering a simple controlled setting involving variable assignment and modular addition. Claim: The transformer learns to compose a Fourier addition circuit with a variable-binding circuit. Supporting result: The combined mechanism is not prescribed, is formally decomposed into Fourier and modified-induction operations, and isolated-subcircuit, mismatch, and intervention tests establish functional composition on unseen combinations. Claim: Assignment and modular-addition mechanisms form in distinct training phases. Supporting result: Checkpoint analysis and alternative-initialization controls show a modular-addition circuit forming before a sharp variable-binding attention reorganization and the compositional accuracy increase.
