audited	w_a6ec62ef7863cd72	Emergence of Foveal Image Sampling from Learning to Attend in Visual Scenes	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1067	230	0	0	Title: Emergence of Foveal Image Sampling from Learning to Attend in Visual Scenes. Claim: A learned 12-by-12 retinal lattice compresses a 100-by-100 visual scene by allocating high resolution only where it is useful. Supporting result: The learnable lattice selects a small differentiable glimpse and reallocates resolution; fixing the lattice worsens error from 5.1% to 11.8% on fixed-size digits and from 24.4% to 31.9% on variable-size digits. Claim: The learned sampling strategy transfers across variable object sizes and visual conditions. Supporting result: The second dataset varies digit size and clutter within the same MNIST visual-search task. Claim: A foveated, eccentricity-dependent sampling geometry emerges from an initially uniform lattice without directly prescribing foveation. Supporting result: The Gaussian-kernel lattice smoothly changes from a uniform grid into a formal eccentricity-dependent geometry; allowing zoom suppresses the geometry, while a fixed-lattice control degrades behavior, tying the learned representation to its function.
