audited	w_667c790db241c1aa	Intrinsic Task Symmetry Drives Generalization in Algorithmic Tasks	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	2219	429	0	0	Title: Intrinsic Task Symmetry Drives Generalization in Algorithmic Tasks. Claim: Grokking, the sudden transition from memorization to generalization, is characterized by the emergence of low-dimensional representations, yet the mechanism underlying this organization remains elusive. Supporting result: Taken together, these observations suggest that geometric organization can be explained through the interplay of in- trinsic task symmetries, sufficient memorization of partial information, and low-dimensional embedding constraints. 8. Claim: Grokking, the sudden transition from memorization to generalization, is characterized by the emergence of low-dimensional representations, yet the mechanism underlying this organization remains elusive. Supporting result: Intrinsic Task Symmetry Drives Generalization in Algorithmic Tasks Hyeonbin Hwang 1 Yeachan Park2 Abstract Grokking, the sudden transition from memoriza- tion to generalization, is characterized by the emergence of low-dimensional representations, yet the mechanism underlying this organization remains elusive. Claim: We propose that intrinsic task symmetries primarily drive grokking and shape the geometry of the model's representation space. Supporting result: We show that gen- eralization emerges during theSymmetry Acquisition phase, which can be tracked using a proposed criterion, and that intrinsic symmetry, together with low-rank rep- resentations, steers the geometric organization of the embedding space. • Accelerating Grokking via Symmetry Alignment: Leveraging these insights, we show that symmetry- and geometry-prompting strategies can accelerate grokking, providing evidence for a causal role of symmetry align- ment in generalization. 2. Claim: We identify a consistent three-stage training dynamic underlying grokking: (i) memorization, (ii) symmetry acquisition, and (iii) geometric organization. Supporting result: Intrinsic Task Symmetry Drives Generalization in Algorithmic Tasks Hyeonbin Hwang 1 Yeachan Park2 Abstract Grokking, the sudden transition from memoriza- tion to generalization, is characterized by the emergence of low-dimensional representations, yet the mechanism underlying this organization remains elusive.
