audited	w_d27b97e00fa37f8b	Prediction horizon shapes representations in predictive learning	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1549	291	0	0	Title: Prediction horizon shapes representations in predictive learning. Claim: Long-horizon prediction learns a low-dimensional state representation that preserves predictive structure. Supporting result: Participation ratio, effective-rank, rank-truncation, and held-out-pair tests show selective concentration into state/action directions sufficient for prediction and associated with better OOD accuracy. Claim: In this work, we identify the prediction horizon as a critical, but often implicit, component of predictive learning objectives. Supporting result: Prediction horizon shapes representations in predictive learning Aviv Ratzon & Omri Barak Technion – Israel Institute of Technology aviv.ratzon@hotmail.com Abstract Predictive learning has emerged as a central paradigm for training models across diverse data domains and is increasingly viewed as a foundation for modern artifi- cial intelligence. Claim: Predictive learning spontaneously recovers the environment's latent manifold. Supporting result: One-hot observations remove prior neighborhood geometry and no manifold regularizer is used; formal OLS/implicit-bias analysis, nonlinear controls, rank truncation, and held-out behavior connect the learned geometry to prediction. Claim: Prediction horizon and depth control recovery of latent task geometry. Supporting result: Horizon, environment size, depth, architecture, and dataset series reorganize hidden states from unstructured solutions to latent manifolds while improving compositional and out-of-distribution prediction.
