audited	w_e1dd76d318faa330	Predictive sequence learning in the hippocampal formation	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1327	233	0	0	Title: Predictive sequence learning in the hippocampal formation. Claim: Temporal prediction compresses redundant sensory inputs into localized sequential representations. Supporting result: Across ten localization networks, predictive hidden states carry more position information than high-dimensional inputs and current-loss controls; for MNIST sequences they retain class/sequence structure while generating the next images and completing withheld suffixes, which non-predictive controls do not. Claim: Sequence training produces a sparse Toeplitz recurrent organization with line-attractor dynamics. Supporting result: Without a prescribed recurrent connectivity pattern, training yields a formally characterized near-Toeplitz/Mexican-hat organization; the recurrent state itself generates ordered replay from noise and completes partial sequences, linking the learned manifold to model behavior. Claim: Error-coded CA1 responses appear rapidly while the CA3 internal representation develops over repeated experience. Supporting result: Training exposure after an environment switch moves the model from immediate CA1 prediction-error coding to a learned CA3 sequence representation; the resulting recurrent organization generates replay and pattern completion, with familiar/noisy-environment and non-predictive controls.
audited	w_8dc658fd6956f31c	Predictive Statistics Shape Emergent World Representations of Grid Walkers	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1794	328	0	0	Title: Predictive Statistics Shape Emergent World Representations of Grid Walkers. Claim: In order to understand this link more precisely, we use a minimal stochastic process as a controlled setting: constrained random walks on a two-dimensional… Supporting result: sufficient statistics of the latent generating distribution needed for prediction. Claim: The probabilistic nature of these models suggests a deep connection between the structure of the world and the geometry of probability distributions. Supporting result: Although demonstrated in a toy system, the results suggest that the geometry of the predictive distribution is a useful lens on how neural networks internalize the structure of their data. 1 Introduction Modern sequence models develop internal representations whose geometric organization often mirrors structure of the world they are trained on [ 1–9]. Claim: Next-token predictors often appear to develop internal representations of the latent world and its rules. Supporting result: That similarly sized RNNs solve the task without adopting these coordinates makes the representation architecture-dependent—a Rashomon effect, in which equally predictive models organize information differently [24–27]: the objective fixes the optimal next-step distribution but not the representation that computes it, and self-attention simply selects a basis in which the world-state update is cheap. 5 Limitations and future challenges Our toy system is deliberately simple, which makes the analytical examination above possible but is also the main limitation: scaling to settings where the predictive structure has no closed form will require a method to decompose complex distributions into tractable parts (see the analysis of [12] for an example where this can be possible).
audited	w_92e74bd4c6d552b0	Pretraining task diversity and the emergence of non-Bayesian in-context learning for regression	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1561	336	0	0	Title: Pretraining task diversity and the emergence of non-Bayesian in-context learning for regression. Claim: We empirically demonstrate a $\textit{task diversity threshold}$ for the emergence of ICL. Supporting result: This study also explores the effect of regularization, model capacity and task structure and underscores, in a concrete example, the critical role of task diversity, alongside data and model scale, in the emergence of ICL. 1 Introduction Pretrained transformers (PTs) can learn new tasks from just a few examples provided in the prompt without taking any gradient steps on those examples [ 1]. Claim: Thus, when pretrained on data with task diversity greater than the threshold, transformers $\textit{can}$ optimally solve fundamentally new tasks in-context. Supporting result: To evaluate its ability to learn new tasks, the PT is tested on the ideal task distribution, TTrue, which is a Gaussian distribution over all latent regression vectors. Claim: This study also explores the effect of regularization, model capacity and task structure and underscores, in a concrete example, the critical role of task… Supporting result: Both methods of increasing dataset size—increasing batch size in Fig. 2 and increasing training steps in this figure—reveal a transition in the behavior of the PT: beyond the task diversity threshold, ICL on new tasks emerges. model with the GPT2 architecture [15] with 8 layers, 128-dimensional embeddings, and 2 attention heads or a small model with 4 layers, 64-dimensional embeddings, and 2 attention heads.
audited	w_ee8ec07c31be9d3c	Progress Measures for Grokking on Real-world Tasks	EXCLUDE	explicit-claims-results+abstract-fallback	1083	217	0	0	Title: Progress Measures for Grokking on Real-world Tasks. Abstract fallback: Grokking, a phenomenon where machine learning models generalize long after overfitting, has been primarily observed and studied in algorithmic tasks. This paper explores grokking in real-world datasets using deep neural networks for classification under the cross-entropy loss. We challenge the prevalent hypothesis that the $L_2$ norm of weights is the primary cause of grokking by demonstrating that grokking can occur outside the expected range of weight norms. To better understand grokking, we introduce three new progress measures: activation sparsity, absolute weight entropy, and approximate local circuit complexity. These measures are conceptually related to generalization and demonstrate a stronger correlation with grokking in real-world datasets compared to weight norms. Our findings suggest that while weight norms might usually correlate with grokking and our progress measures, they are not causative, and our proposed measures provide a better understanding of the dynamics of grokking.
audited	w_f3172e652cebe4b6	Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1956	361	0	0	Title: Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability. Claim: The generalizing computation compresses modular addition into a small set of Fourier directions and a sparse circuit. Supporting result: Five key frequencies explain 95% of logit variance; ten sine/cosine directions support low loss; ablating a key frequency destroys performance while ablating the other roughly 95% of frequencies does not and can improve loss. Claim: Grokking is a transition between memorizing and generalizing organizational regimes linked to a rapid behavioral change. Supporting result: At a distinct training interval, test loss drops while Fourier-component sparsity rises sharply during cleanup; the corresponding trajectories are reproduced for four additional random seeds and checked under architecture, modulus, and regularization variations. Claim: The Fourier mechanism generalizes across modular-arithmetic variants and other algorithmic tasks. Supporting result: The primary test split contains unseen input pairs from the same modular-addition task; other primes, architectures, and algorithmic tasks are separately trained experiments. Claim: The transformer learns a Fourier/trigonometric coordinate system for modular addition that was not prescribed by the architecture. Supporting result: Weights and activations implement addition as rotations in learned frequency coordinates; the structure is mathematically reconstructed and causally tested by Fourier-space ablations, and generalizing runs learn variants of the same algorithm. Claim: Training progression produces a qualitative reorganization from memorization to a sparse Fourier circuit and then cleanup, enabling generalization. Supporting result: Training epoch is logged against restricted/excluded loss, Fourier sparsity, and behavior; the circuit-formation and cleanup phases reorganize the learned computation and precede or accompany test generalization across five seeds.
