audited	w_7afa59979906b85f	What Needs to Go Right for an Induction Head? A Mechanistic Study of In-Context Learning Circuits and Their Formation	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1867	355	0	0	Title: What Needs to Go Right for an Induction Head? A Mechanistic Study of In-Context Learning Circuits and Their Formation. Claim: The induction mechanism represents its computation in a simpler, more efficient internal code selected and used by the model. Supporting result: Ablations and clamping decompose the learned computation into three interacting subcircuits, while multiple induction heads show redundancy and additive contributions. Claim: Interacting induction subcircuits cause a sharp training transition from chance-level behavior to near-perfect in-context generalization. Supporting result: Loss and accuracy change sharply at the same training interval that induction strength rises. Clamping the match/copy components throughout training sharpens, advances, or removes the phase profile, causally showing how three smoothly learned subcircuits generate the discontinuity. Data controls shift the transition and the main mechanism replicates over five model initializations. Claim: The learned induction circuit transfers its abstraction to genuinely unfamiliar task families. Supporting result: The model generalizes to unseen Omniglot exemplars and unseen label pairs, demonstrating genuine held-out performance within the constructed 2-way 1-shot ICL problem. Claim: Training progression and controlled increases in task complexity reorganize the induction circuit and shift when in-context learning forms. Supporting result: Sequences seen is an explicit training control variable: a loss plateau gives way to near-perfect test accuracy while induction heads form. Increasing class count or label count delays distinct subcircuits and the full transition, and training-time clamping isolates the mechanisms producing those shifts. Central subcircuit results hold across five initialization seeds and scaling trends reproduce across most seeds.
audited	w_33b529766cc60dd2	What One Cannot, Two Can: Two-Layer Transformers Provably Represent Induction Heads on Any-Order Markov Chains	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1178	228	0	0	Title: What One Cannot, Two Can: Two-Layer Transformers Provably Represent Induction Heads on Any-Order Markov Chains. Claim: A two-layer, one-head-per-layer transformer compactly represents any conditional k-gram through a dyadic context code and matching operation. Supporting result: The constructive proof encodes the length-k context in the first layer and matches it in the second with one head per layer and compact parameter/precision bounds, avoiding the exponential-width one-layer alternative. Claim: The learned induction mechanism generalizes to arbitrary Markov order k. Supporting result: The theorem supplies a k-dependent construction for any order, but the gradient-descent emergence analysis is restricted to a simplified first-order model under staged optimal-component assumptions. Claim: Increasing depth from one to two layers creates a qualitatively new internal organization that efficiently implements higher-order induction. Supporting result: Depth is the explicit variable: one layer requires exponential width, while two single-head layers implement distinct context-encoding and context-matching stages and recover the conditional k-gram behavior.
audited	w_5362be8fefcfa6c2	When and Where: A Model Hippocampal Network Unifies Formation of Time Cells and Place Cells	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1034	199	0	0	Title: When and Where: A Model Hippocampal Network Unifies Formation of Time Cells and Place Cells. Claim: Masked reconstruction learns distinct place-cell-like and time-cell-like recurrent representations. Supporting result: Uniformly initialized recurrence develops formally measured spatial or sequential temporal codes without those hidden coordinates being assigned; the recurrent state predicts fully masked intervals, and recurrent-matrix replacement plus input/output randomization isolates the learned recurrent regime as the source of sequential coding while reconstruction readout establishes functional use. Claim: Changing the balance and availability of spatial and temporal inputs reorganizes the hidden representation. Supporting result: Event-duration, spatial-mask, and fixed-dimension channel-ratio series move the same predictive autoencoder between place-like, mixed, and time-like population regimes while it reconstructs withheld sensory trajectories; pure spatial and temporal tasks serve as endpoint controls.
