audited	w_b1b2841abfaf8ef2	Which Attention Heads Matter for In-Context Learning?	INCLUDE	explicit-claims-results	1838	356	0	0	Title: Which Attention Heads Matter for In-Context Learning?. Claim: A small set of function-vector heads contains a compact task representation sufficient to recover much of ICL behavior. Supporting result: Preserving the top 2% FV heads preserves ICL accuracy under broader ablation; summing selected task-conditioned head outputs into one function vector and injecting it into shuffled prompts recovers most clean-run accuracy for all but Pythia 2.8B. Claim: A phase-like transition from induction to function-vector organization produces the emergence of ICL performance. Supporting result: Induction score rises sharply, but FV score and ICL accuracy are reported to increase gradually; individual-head transitions are gradual and no critical point is replicated across seeds or controls. Claim: The FV-head mechanism generalizes across tasks and model families. Supporting result: The study separates tasks used to score heads from held-out ICL evaluation tasks and repeats across models, but task vectors are still extracted from same-task demonstrations within the ICL paradigm. Claim: Function-vector heads constitute a novel learned representational basis for abstract ICL tasks. Supporting result: The paper defines task vectors as analyst-selected sums of mean residual-stream head outputs; it explicitly labels the interpretation that FV heads perform more abstract computation as speculative. Claim: Increasing model size and training time reorganizes important ICL heads from induction-like heads toward function-vector heads, with the latter increasingly supporting few-shot ICL. Supporting result: Model size and training checkpoint are explicit variables; FV-head ablation becomes more behaviorally consequential in larger models, and tracked individual heads lose induction score while gaining FV score during training.
